// SHIDWORD / SLOBYTE / SLODWORD are NOT functions.
//
// These three were declared `int X(...);` like everything around them, and that
// form could never be defined usefully: the linker asks for `?SLODWORD@@YAHZZ`,
// which an ellipsis-only body can produce, but such a body cannot READ its
// argument (no named parameter to hand to va_start), so it could only ever
// return a constant. Every one of the 43 call sites in the tree passes an lvalue
// that is meant to be read in place -- the low word of a 64-bit local, or the
// low byte of a float -- so a constant would be a silent wrong
// answer rather than a missing one.
//
// They are IDA's accessors, and Runtime/IdaMacros.h already carries LODWORD and
// HIDWORD in exactly this form: an address-taking cast, an lvalue. The three
// below complete the family, so the call sites keep working and the symbol
// disappears instead of being stubbed. Written as macros for the same reason
// IdaMacros.h writes its own that way -- `BYTE4(x) = y;` has to stay assignable.
//
// The argument is evaluated once, so an lvalue is required; all 43 call sites
// pass one.
#define SLODWORD(x) (*(int           *)&(x))
#define SHIDWORD(x) (*(int           *)((unsigned char *)&(x) + 4))
#define SLOBYTE(x)  (*(signed  char  *)&(x))

